#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167867} :activity/id #uuid "f8370aa1-a1ec-583a-aa70-e26a8cb55550" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050600 :topic/id #uuid "5771ac42-6250-50b5-b69b-5b90a029c140" :topic/math-academy-id 3191} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168012 :tutorial/content "The **Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model** describes the interaction between two species (predator and prey) and is given by the nonlinear system of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, \n\n- $x=x(t)$ is the *prey population*, and\n\n- $y=y(t)$ is the *predator population*.\n\nRemember that a derivative represents a *rate of change*.\n\n- In the first equation, $x' = ax - bxy,$ the prey changes due to the following:\n\n- The *intrinsic growth*, which is given by the $ax$ term. If there are no predators ($y=0$), then $x' = ax > 0$ and the prey grows exponentially.\n\n- The *predation*, which is given by the $-bxy$ term. When prey and predators interact, the term $-bxy$ decreases $x.$\n\n- In the second equation, $y' = -cy + dxy,$ the predators change due to the following:\n\n- The *intrinsic decay*, which is given by the $-cy$ term. If there is no prey ($x=0$), then $y' = -cy < 0$ and predators decline exponentially.\n\n- The *feeding/reproduction*, which is given by the $dxy$ term. When prey and predators interact, the term $dxy$ increases $y.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "df04c110-07af-507a-9a45-8cef3122f2d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14531 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ca5cf22c-180f-58ff-8262-fb95f4fb6109" :step/math-academy-id 589726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166557 :question/id #uuid "2266e14f-c662-5844-bfb4-3b4259f9232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445110}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"} :step/id #uuid "a7a45fab-41bd-5b22-9d1d-e4566baed3a5" :step/math-academy-id 589727 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166554 :question/id #uuid "037b7db9-131c-5a08-aa84-d7552a903e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-21726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557220}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"} :step/id #uuid "c0b5125d-8295-5d89-bd4b-316c26197a12" :step/math-academy-id 589730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168013 :tutorial/content "Consider the Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants, $x=x(t)$ is the prey population, and $y=y(t)$ is the predator population.\n\nTo classify the equilibrium at the origin, we need to find the corresponding Jacobian matrix.\n\nLet's denote $f_1(x,y)=ax-bxy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-cy+dxy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(ax-bxy)=a-by,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ax-bxy)=-bx \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-cy+dxy)=dy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-cy+dxy)=-c+dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/e1/e1c9a7c83c6c5790b264670bba4c8e7352ac18239b737348a85c393b5de3be6e.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/7a/7a3543ed5d9733302a80e87b344d4f74d46d9bc27d5a034692b81cec54a56635.svg) to get\n\n![](images/24/24ac6a3fb192074570c1bf62585a5b8e57e6052b6118159c8f1645a31d92b21f.svg)\n\nSince this matrix is diagonal, the eigenvalues are simply the diagonal entries: $\\lambda_1 = a$ and $\\lambda_2 = -c.$ Because $a,c$ are positive constants, we have $\\lambda_1 > 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*." :tutorial/id #uuid "36f1f197-5258-587f-b119-95ff77bb685a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14532 :tutorial/title "Local Behavior Near the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "41a5a760-4fcc-5875-91b1-93493893b0f7" :step/math-academy-id 589733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166555 :question/id #uuid "47c1e08d-d5b4-5f1e-b225-d1336645898f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21727"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1fa1647-1acb-50bd-95e7-26ed0c495bd4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557273}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Lotka-Volterra Model"} :step/id #uuid "6b342954-1db0-5d02-a6a8-84bfa7a0897d" :step/math-academy-id 589734 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168014 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider a typical curved trajectory in more detail.\n\n![](images/73/7330af149428dd84fa1e94fd914a1e71d074f38bfe4ced44928a3c7043ee4fe9.png)\n\nIt can be split into four parts by the lines $x=\\dfrac{c}{d}$ and $y=\\dfrac{a}{b}$ passing through the nonzero equilibrium:\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey *decreases* and the number of predators *increases*.\n\n- From $B$ to $C,$ the number of prey *decreases* and the number of predators *decreases*.\n\n- From $C$ to $D,$ the number of prey *increases* and the number of predators *decreases*.\n\n- From $D$ to $A,$ the number of prey *increases* and the number of predators *increases*.\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d04db41fdc9fd8940dec3a130c4dc2a26bd4b3fa3861f40f65b0c708cb16084.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "310e1e78-8811-5cee-b6ef-0c42f8ff1481" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14533 :tutorial/title "Population Dynamics Graph"} :step/id #uuid "75643098-713e-56c0-9776-9d37e4b4c88a" :step/math-academy-id 589737 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166556 :question/id #uuid "98a0cd06-80a8-514c-bc89-c739d8502fda" :question/math-academy-id "e-21728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445108}] :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"} :step/id #uuid "7accc131-3365-5964-b248-f0c1fe8fe597" :step/math-academy-id 589738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168026 :tutorial/content "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthe equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}ax-bxy &=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-bxy & =0 \\\\ x(a-by) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\dfrac{a}{b}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+d(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\dfrac{a}{b},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{a}{b}(-c+dx) & =0 \\\\ -c+dx & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{c}{d}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, there are two equilibria:\n\n- The *origin* $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{(0,0)}.$\n\n- The *nonzero equilibrium* $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\,\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4f47199-6618-59ac-aa11-c5d0bc8ff50f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14592 :tutorial/title "Equilibrium Points"} :step/id #uuid "9636d5a8-52d6-57c2-8a64-64ff786d40da" :step/math-academy-id 589752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168027 :tutorial/content "Next, we evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/d5/d57e5d0566616dcf983c8d2d9ee8375cb70aa44d4d5933026266396202248d22.svg)\nat the nonzero equilibrium ![](images/b3/b3e4462dae63d3e4cbaddcd890e1553dd26372c2fdca8efe87cbfb8984b9a492.svg) to get\n\n![](images/ec/ecc7fb063630624a8acc4ab201208c2cfb86fe97b910cdc6e17fb71629299385.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -\\frac{bc}{d} \\\\ \\frac{ad}{b} & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )\\cdot (-\\lambda )-(-\\frac{bc}{d})\\cdot \\frac{ad}{b} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+ac & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm \\sqrt{ac}\\,\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a *stable center*.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model, it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues.\nHowever, we will not go into detail on why in this lesson.\n\nJust keep in mind that for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone would be inconclusive in this case (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source).\n\nTherefore, a typical phase portrait of the system looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/23/236a29ae07d8fd091fd602ad124af53b49754419b949bd42713078bc9d8814b6.png)\n\nThe global picture in the first quadrant is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions which intersect at the saddle point $(0,0){:}$\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach infinity (unstable line). Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey increases exponentially.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin (stable line). Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories circle around the nonzero equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73373dc9-48c3-5ba7-b785-32651f0f7563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14593 :tutorial/title "Local Behavior Near the Nonzero Equilibrium"} :step/id #uuid "fce4e25e-7cdb-503e-b918-2448bb55d8cc" :step/math-academy-id 589781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167871}}] :activity/title "The Lotka-Volterra Predator-Prey Model" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166989}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496376} :activity/id #uuid "d7166fa4-3c38-59ac-b04f-d18e2da6957a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050600 :topic/id #uuid "5771ac42-6250-50b5-b69b-5b90a029c140" :topic/math-academy-id 3191} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7ca91be-1147-5049-8064-d763eb6133e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370183} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f4acca5-dac1-54fa-9e21-9915bff82215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412cc220-b5ee-5f02-87a4-33b233723962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370185} :answer-field/id #uuid "96278b61-4187-511e-8e54-0a1a2d062155" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ef04316-d6de-5c0d-a680-25266abf9b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-334998" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=6x-5xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-7y+3xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-5xy &=0 \\\\ -7y+3xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-5xy & =0 \\\\ x(6-5y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{6}{5}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7y+3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{6}{5},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-7+3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(-7+3x) & =0 \\\\ -7+3x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{6}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{6}{5}).$"} :step/id #uuid "0eed59e7-77cc-5786-81f0-e4a3560f8518" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f5c3169-d779-521a-8197-1104e5c087d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495867} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a964f9-cc26-5d58-9dc8-4e4f3ec7ff0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164545} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18a65b5-78bf-5bcc-a5ae-bc42d6062064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495871} :answer-field/id #uuid "eecf0f04-faa2-5ba0-be4b-55b15c49c548" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1666d90-74ae-56ff-b146-0bf87963aa16" :question/math-academy-id "q-334640" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=2x-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3xy &=0 \\\\ -5y+4xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3xy & =0 \\\\ x(2-3y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y+4(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{3},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-5+4x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(-5+4x) & =0 \\\\ -5+4x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{2}{3}).$"} :step/id #uuid "b096df3e-830d-5b6c-8579-d38d657cdeef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71e64735-7edd-5494-af06-bbbb33ad6243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :d