b/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370237} {:answer/id #uuid "9e09dc0c-6ad4-5e93-80e0-5b63815671cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370238} {:answer/id #uuid "f995075a-ec4a-54dc-8d31-72b28d14d079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370236} :answer-field/id #uuid "53efb467-1771-57c2-a34a-f98af9fc7bb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "238e2eda-b356-520d-9329-99af591a6032" :question/math-academy-id "q-335008" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=4x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-3y+5xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2xy)=4-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+5xy)=5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+5xy)=-3+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}4-2y & -2x \\\\ 5y & -3+5x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}4-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 5(0) & -3+5(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fdb3e243-a44e-5cfb-8bf4-f284a98094ba"}] :activity/title "The Lotka-Volterra Predator-Prey Model" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495507}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514533} :activity/id #uuid "41863a24-f3a0-5f1c-8287-b8fe419e3c11" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050600 :topic/id #uuid "5771ac42-6250-50b5-b69b-5b90a029c140" :topic/math-academy-id 3191} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60581416-ee86-55cf-8c4b-1d603d6e36dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513791} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae6bdd6-8495-56ce-a1de-f32c180827b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513855} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e4fd3f-ac7c-5453-9f0c-7e59971b180a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514044} {:answer/id #uuid "c54a96