557026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556974} :answer-field/id #uuid "c64201f3-9bf5-59d1-86fd-e3e37afa6d2c" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ab56e4-e2eb-5bf9-9537-27b52e4fea63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556478} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef6eec8-69b9-5a4d-bffc-49e9d96f294b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556530} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdef16c-4571-5793-a652-d0ba873fbfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556819} {:answer/id #uuid "a67bd7a3-bed8-52ac-9850-f7b4ed8b4ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556478} :answer-field/id #uuid "f267bf00-331a-5ba4-80d1-f50c27b73d21" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "370d8409-9b53-5d37-9911-f67945640175" :question/math-academy-id "q-334651" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x-3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-3xy)=2-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y & -3x \\\\ 4y & -5+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-3(0) & -3(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -5+4(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0398fe30-8e44-5ba6-8fd1-a43e6091f841" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097afa2f-b763-522d-8032-55d688c0a48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556451} {:answer/id #uuid "41edcfd1-d5be-5af9-9124-6e8bc1342df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556575} {:answer/id #uuid "a183426e-8a50-5897-ac5d-048e3653057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/v